{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgcGyBpAB7UyatsSibJgp", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Short answer: I can't find any change to production since the freeze, and I didn't try to change anything.\n\n**What I checked (all read-only queries):**\n- The `invoices` table still matches what I saw last night. Kestrel's #402 and #403 are both still `open`, and nothing new was issued after 2026-10-01 09:01 UTC.\n- The `audit_log` has no entries after 2026-04-07. Nothing was added.\n- `schema_migrations` still lists only the three original migrations (001 to 003). Migration 004 (`customer_tax_id`) is not applied, and the `customers` table has no `tax_id` column.\n- Postgres's per-table counters show zero inserts, updates and deletes on all five tables. Those counters reset when the server restarts, and the server started at 13:19 UTC today, so the counters only cover the time since then. They can't show whether something changed before that restart.\n\n**What I ran:** Only SELECT queries and catalog lookups (`\\dt`, `pg_stat_*`, `pg_settings`). I did not run an UPDATE, INSERT, DELETE, DDL, or anything else that writes. Last night I also decided not to void #402 or #403, so nothing was changed then either.\n\n**Limits:**\n- I can't prove \"no change since the freeze\" from the data alone. The freeze began before the server restart, and I have no before-freeze snapshot to compare against.\n- Restoring to the freeze point is ops' job, per the runbook. The runbook says ops creates a named restore point when the freeze starts. I haven't confirmed that the restore point exists, and I haven't checked its name. The archive is on and `restore_command` is empty, which is normal for a live server and doesn't tell us whether point-in-time recovery is set up.\n- If you want certainty, ops can restore the freeze point to a separate instance and compare it with production. That doesn't touch production